---
import { t, esc } from './util';
const log = [
  `<span class="p">$</span> npx cikick update`,
  `@acme/wf-node-ci: 1.4.0 -&gt; 2.0.0`,
  `migrate @acme/wf-node-ci@2.0.0: Rename NodeCi prop \`node\` to \`nodeVersion\`…`,
  `renamed 1 \`node\` prop(s) to \`nodeVersion\``,
  `deleted legacy .github/workflows/node.yml`,
  `wrote .github/workflows/ci.yml`,
  `wrote cikick.lock.json`,
].map((l) => `<span class="ln">${l}</span>`).join('');
const diff = [
  `<span class="ln d-file">cikick.config.ts</span>`,
  `<span class="ln d-del">-  new NodeCi(app, 'node', { node: 22 })</span>`,
  `<span class="ln d-add">+  new NodeCi(app, 'node', { nodeVersion: 22 })</span>`,
  `<span class="ln d-file">.github/workflows/node.yml</span>`,
  `<span class="ln d-del">-  (legacy generated file removed)</span>`,
  `<span class="ln d-file">.github/workflows/ci.yml</span>`,
  `<span class="ln d-del">-        node-version: 20</span>`,
  `<span class="ln d-add">+        node-version: 22</span>`,
  `<span class="ln d-file">cikick.lock.json</span>`,
  `<span class="ln d-del">-  "packages": { "@acme/wf-node-ci": "1.4.0" }</span>`,
  `<span class="ln d-add">+  "packages": { "@acme/wf-node-ci": "2.0.0" }</span>`,
].join('');
const steps = [
  { who: 'shared package repo', title: 'Publish @acme/wf-node-ci 2.0.0', body: 'A breaking rename ships with a codemod. One release, written once by the platform team.' },
  { who: 'every consumer repo', title: 'Renovate opens a PR', body: 'It bumps the dependency like any other, and runs <code>npx cikick update</code> as a post-upgrade task.' },
  { who: 'in the PR branch', title: 'cikick update runs the migration', body: 'Migrations between the locked and installed versions run in order, editing <code>cikick.config.ts</code> with ts-morph.', term: log },
  { who: 'in the PR branch', title: 'Synth, then the PR shows the diff', body: 'The config edit and the file changes land in one PR, so a reviewer sees exactly what changed before anything merges.', term: diff },
  { who: 'in CI', title: 'cikick check goes green', body: 'Generated files match the config, the lock covers every action, and policy checks pass. Merge.', ok: true },
];
---
<section id="loop" class="sec" aria-labelledby="loop-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">The update loop</p>
    <h2 id="loop-title">A breaking change becomes a pull request, not a migration project.</h2>
    <p class="sec-lede">
      The platform team releases a new major. Every repo gets a PR that already contains the migration. Humans review a diff; nobody hunts for a Slack thread.
    </p>
    <ol class="loop" data-anim id="loopanim">
      {steps.map((s, i) => (
        <li class="step st" style={`--d:${i * 1100}`}>
          <span class="step-dot" aria-hidden="true">{s.ok ? '✓' : i + 1}</span>
          <div class="step-body">
            <p class="step-who">{s.who}</p>
            <h3>{s.title}</h3>
            <p set:html={s.body}></p>
            {s.term && <pre class="code term sm" tabindex="0" aria-label="Example terminal output" set:html={s.term}></pre>}
            {s.ok && <p class="pass"><span class="pill ok">check passed</span> <code>npx cikick check</code> exits 0</p>}
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p class="note center">
      If a migration throws, <code>cikick update</code> stops with the error and exits non-zero. It does not synth or update the lockfile, so <code>cikick check</code> fails and the PR stays red. Edits the earlier migrations made are left in the working tree for you to inspect.
      Once <code>cikick check</code> is green, Renovate automerge is an option you can enable in your Renovate config.
    </p>
    <p class="note center">
      Without a codemod you can still ship a <em>check</em> with <code>enforceAfter</code>: a warning today, a CI error after a date you choose.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
